            <ArticleTitle>Association of neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio and microalbuminuria in patients with normal eGFR.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1515/rjim-2017-0036</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/rjim.ahead-of-print/rjim-2017-0036/rjim-2017-0036.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to evaluate the association between neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio (NLR), platelet-to-lymphocyte ratio (PLR) and microalbuminuria in patients with normal estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">174 patients who had eGFR≥60 ml/min/1.73 m2 were studied. Patients were divided into two groups according to the urinary albumin excretion as microalbuminuric group (n=105) and normoalbuminuric group (n=69). NLR and PLR levels were calculated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">NLR was significantly higher (p˂0.05) in microalbuminuric patients (1.91±0.70) compared with normoalbuminuric patients (1.63±0.53). A positive correlation was found between urine albumin excretion and NLR in whole study group (r=0.214, p˂0.005).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Higher NLR levels were found in microalbuminuric patients with normal eGFR. Also a significant positive correlation was observed between albuminuria and NLR.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
